TEXT="unconditional default distribution"></node><node TEXT="conditional default distribution"></node></node><node TEXT="credit risk portfolio models"><node TEXT="CreditRisk+(credit suisse)"></node><node TEXT="CreditRiskTM (JPMorgan)"></node><node TEXT="KMV model (Moody)"></node><node TEXT="Credit Portfolio View (McKinsey)"></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="management of credit risk"><node TEXT="credit derivative swap"><node TEXT="First-to-Default CDS"></node><node TEXT="Total Return Swap (TRS)"></node><node TEXT="Asset-Backed Credit-Linked Notes"></node><node TEXT="Vulnerable Option"></node><node TEXT="Swap with Credit Risk"></node><node TEXT="Collateralized Debt Obligations (CDOs)"></node></node><node TEXT="structured products"><node TEXT="PD and correlation effect"></node><node TEXT="convexity effect"></node></node><node TEXT="securitization"><node TEXT="seven frictions"></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="operation risk management and measurement" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="OpRisk management framework"><node TEXT="def"><node TEXT="risk of loss resulting from inadequate or failed internal processes, people and systems or from eternal events"></node><node TEXT="include legal risk, but exclude strategic and reputational risk"></node></node><node TEXT="three common lines of defense"><node TEXT="business line management"></node><node TEXT="independent corporate operational risk management function"></node><node TEXT="independent review"></node></node><node TEXT="principles for sound management of operational risk"><node TEXT="summary"><node TEXT="人和流程"></node></node><node TEXT="general principle"><node TEXT="1. The board of directors should take the lead in establishing a strong risk management culture"></node><node TEXT="2. Banks should develop, implement and maintain a Framework that is fully integrated into the bank’s overall risk management processes"></node></node><node TEXT="governance"><node TEXT="board of directors"><node TEXT="3. The board of directors should establish, approve and periodically review the Framework"></node><node TEXT="4. The board of directors should approve and review a risk appetite and tolerance statement for operational risk that articulates the nature, types, and levels of operational risk that the bank is willing to assume"></node></node><node TEXT="senior management"><node TEXT="5. Senior management should develop for approval by the board of directors a clear, effective and robust governance structure with well defined, transparent and consistent lines of responsibility"></node></node></node><node TEXT="risk management enviroment"><node TEXT="indentification and assessment"><node TEXT="6. Senior management should ensure the identification and assessment of the operational risk inherent in all material products, activities, processes and systems to make sure the inherent risks and incentives are well understood"><node TEXT="tools for assessing operating risks"><node TEXT="audit findings"><node TEXT="focus on control weaknesses and vulnerabilities"></node></node><node TEXT="internal loss data collection and analysis"><node TEXT="provide meaningful information for assessing exposure to operational risk and effectiveness of internal controls"></node></node><node TEXT="external data collection and analysis"></node><node TEXT="risk self assessment(RSA)"><node TEXT="assess the processes underlying its operations against potential threats, vulnerabilities, and consider their potential impact"></node></node><node TEXT="risk control self assessment(RCSA)"><node TEXT="evaluate inherent risk before controls are considered"></node></node><node TEXT="business process mapping"></node><node TEXT="risk and performance indicators"><node TEXT="key risk indicators(KRIs)"></node><node TEXT="key performance indicators(KPIs)"></node></node><node TEXT="scenario analysis"></node><node TEXT="measurement"><node TEXT="use the output of the risk assessment tools as inputs into a model that estimates operational risk exposure"></node></node><node TEXT="comparative analysis"></node></node></node><node TEXT="7. Senior management should ensure that there is an approval process for all new products, activities, processes and systems that fully assesses operational risk"></node></node><node TEXT="monitoring and reporting"><node TEXT="8. Senior management should ensure that there is an approval process for all new products, activities, processes and systems that fully assesses operational risk"></node></node><node TEXT="control and mitigation"><node TEXT="9. Banks should have a strong control environment that utilizes policies, processes and systems; appropriate internal controls; and appropriate risk mitigation and transfer strategies"><node TEXT="five components of strong control enviroment"><node TEXT="control enviroment"></node><node TEXT="risk assessment"></node><node TEXT="control activities"></node><node TEXT="information and communication"></node><node TEXT="monitoring activities"></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="business resiliency and continuity"><node TEXT="10. Banks should have business resiliency and continuity plans in place to ensure an ability to operate on an ongoing basis and limit losses in the event of severe business disruption"></node></node></node><node TEXT="role of disclosure"><node TEXT="11. A bank’s public disclosures should allow stakeholders to assess its approach to operational risk management"></node></node></node><node TEXT="enterprise risk management"><node TEXT="def"><node TEXT="all risks viewed together within a coordinated and strategic framework"></node><node TEXT="macro level"><node TEXT="more strategic risks and opportunities should be put into core business"></node></node><node TEXT="micro level"><node TEXT="focus on decentralizing the risk-return trade-off in a company"></node></node></node><node TEXT="four-step conceptual framework"><node TEXT="management begins by determining the firm&apos;s appetite"></node><node TEXT="given the fi